// The threaded schedule on WoSX's default tree with persistent workers:
// fcpwb-threads.bonsai's parfor over the points handed to sixteen workers,
// one per hardware thread of the performance cores compare.sh pins to,
// that claim points from a counter until none are left (`persistent`,
// Aila and Laine's persistent threads), the workers `t` bound to the CPU
// threads -- what fcpw4w16-persistent.bonsai is on the four-wide tree.
// Against fcpwb-threads it measures the dynamic claim's worth on this
// tree, whose nodes are smaller and whose depth is twice the wide tree's.
// compare.sh runs it as a threaded schedule.
import apps/wosx/schedules/fcpwb;

schedule {
    solve_all.persistent(i, t, 16);
    solve_all.bind(t, CPUThread);
}
